INT. NATIONAL RESPONSE CALL CENTER, FLOOR THREE - DAY

Beige cubicles. Gray carpet. A banner: "EVERY CALL IS AN OPPORTUNITY!" A wall clock ticks over a whiteboard of call counts.

DUANE PRESCOTT, 40s, in a headset and a tie loosened to the second button, sits at a cube with a script taped to the monitor. SHANICE WILLIAMS, 30s, in the next cube, fills out a form in tiny block letters, never glancing up.

                    DUANE
          Thank you for calling National Response, my name is Duane. How may I...

He stops. Listens. Writes a line. Crosses it out.

                    DUANE (CONT'D)
          Yes, ma'am, I understand your frustration.

Shanice lowers her pen.

                    SHANICE
          Duane. You've said "I understand" eleven times.

                    DUANE
          Because I do understand.

                    SHANICE
          You've been transferred to nobody.

A chirp from the ceiling speaker. The intercom.

                    KOVACS (O.S.)
          Attention, floor three. This is a reminder that average handle time is up four percent. Please be mindful.

                    DUANE
          (muttering)
          Mindful. I'm mindful.

                    SHANICE
          (to the room)
          Four percent. We're a ship and it's four percent.

A few muffled snorts from nearby cubes. Duane slides his chair back. Shanice stands. They look at each other over the cubicle wall, then at the clock.

                    KOVACS (O.S.)
          Also, Duane. Shanice. Please remember that personal conversations are logged.

Duane takes off his headset and places it in the middle of his desk. Shanice does the same. Together they push back their chairs, which squeal on the carpet.

                    DUANE
          (to the ceiling)
          Mr. Kovacs, I have a question.

                    KOVACS (O.S.)
          Please direct it to your supervisor.

                    DUANE
          You are my supervisor.

                    KOVACS (O.S.)
          Then please direct it to a future me.

Shanice walks to the banner and takes a corner in each fist. The tape tears loose with a long squeal. Duane takes hold of the other end. They bring it down and stand there in a slump of paper, holding it between them.

                    SHANICE
          I always hated that banner.

                    DUANE
          I wrote it.

                    SHANICE
          I know.

The intercom chirps again, but neither of them listens.
